<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Head to Toes! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Head to Toes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Interpret simple phrases from a text.
    Communication: Describe everyday objects using visual support.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret simple phrases from a text by pointing to related visuals (e.g., points to "head" when hearing "Touch your head.").
    Can describe everyday objects using visual support (e.g., when asked, "What do you wear on rainy days?" the student responds, "I wear my red raincoat." When shown a picture and asked, "What's this?" the student responds, "It's my red raincoat.").
    Can use simple gestures to clarify descriptions for peers (e.g., points to "hand" and says "hand").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game. "Simon says: Touch your head!", "Simon says: Wiggle your toes!", "Simon says: Put on your hat!" (mime). The teacher uses target body parts (head, toes, hand, arm, leg, knee) and clothing items (hat, shoes, coat, socks).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the commands clearly, using gestures and pointing to their own body parts or miming wearing clothes. For "Put on your hat," the teacher mimes putting a hat on their head, clearly articulating /h/ in "hat". For "Touch your toes," the teacher touches their toes, emphasizing /t/ in "toes".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions and actions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to elbow) "Is this my knee?" (Students shake heads "No" or say "No, elbow.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a shoe) "Do I wear my shoe on my head?" (Students say "No, on my foot.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher presents a simple, illustrated set of instructions for "How to Play Our Body & Clothes Matching Game" (the 21st Century Project). The instructions are displayed on a poster or projector with clear pictures. Each step is a simple phrase. E.g., "1. Show a body card: This is a hand. 2. Show a clothes card: This is a glove. 3. Match them: Put the glove on the hand. 4. Say: I wear the glove on my hand."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated instructions for "Body & Clothes Matching Game." This input serves as a concrete text that students will learn to interpret and then re-present/mediate to their peers. It's a short notice/instructions with pictures, suitable for Pre-A1.4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying body parts and clothing: "This is my hand." "The hat is on my head." Following directions: "Put on your hat."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads each instruction, points to the corresponding picture, and asks students to mime the action or point to the relevant body part/clothing.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we do first? Show a body card!" (Students point to the picture of a body card.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where does the hat go? On the head!" (Students point to their heads.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpret simple phrases from a text, describe everyday objects using visual support, use simple gestures to clarify descriptions for peers.
                Grammar: Imperative + noun ("Put on your hat."), WH-questions ("Where is your hat?"), Present simple ("This is my head.").
                Vocabulary: All listed nouns, verbs, adjectives, demonstratives, possessive adjectives, prepositions related to body parts and clothing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen, Point, and Repeat (Controlled practice):</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of body parts and clothing. Teacher says a word (e.g., "head," "hat"), students point to the corresponding card and repeat the word. Then, the teacher gives simple commands like "Touch your nose," "Put on your socks" (mime), students perform the action and say the word. Focus on /h/ in "hand/hat" and /t/ in "toes."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Description Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in small groups with sets of "Body & Clothes Matching Game" cards. One student picks a card (e.g., a "foot" card) and says "This is my foot." The next student picks a matching clothing card (e.g., "shoe") and says "This is my shoe. I wear my shoe on my foot." They must use a demonstrative and a possessive adjective. The teacher circulates, asking "What's this?" and "Where do you wear it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Gesture and Explain (Pair interaction/Peer check):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of "Body & Clothes Matching Game" cards. Student A takes a card (e.g., "arm") and says "This is my arm." Student B picks a matching clothes card (e.g., "jacket") and says "This is my jacket. I wear my jacket on my arm." They use gestures (pointing to their arm, miming putting on a jacket) to clarify their descriptions to their peer. They then swap roles. This activity directly prepares them for explaining their game.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> Students, in their small groups, will demonstrate and explain how to play their "Body & Clothes Matching Game" to another group or the whole class. They will use the cards they have created (Project 1). This involves mediating the visual information on the cards into clear verbal descriptions and instructions for their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group demonstration and oral explanation/presentation. Each student in the group takes turns presenting a match and explaining it. One student holds up a body card (e.g., "head") and another holds up a clothing card (e.g., "hat").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students will actively use the vocabulary of body parts and clothing to explain the matches in their game. They will explain rules using simple imperatives and present simple sentences (e.g., "Put the hat on the head. This is my head. I wear my hat."). They will use gestures and pointing to clarify, embodying the mediation skill.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on Fluency:</b> Encourage students to speak clearly and confidently as they explain their game. The emphasis is on completing the task of explaining the game and its matches, using the target language and gestures, rather than perfect grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist. The teacher observes each group during their game demonstration, noting their ability to interpret and explain the cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students play their "Body & Clothes Matching Game" in small groups. The teacher listens as students explain their matches and use simple phrases and gestures to clarify for their peers. For example, a student shows "hand" card and "glove" card, says "This is my hand. I wear a glove on my hand," and points to their hand and the cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of body parts (e.g., /h/ in "hand") and clothing vocabulary (e.g., /t/ in "hat").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 5-7 body parts and 5-7 clothing items from the lesson.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in explanation:</b> Can clearly explain why cards match (e.g., "I wear the hat on my head.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of gestures:</b> Uses simple gestures (pointing, miming) to clarify descriptions for peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works well with peers during the game and explanation.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a happy face or a sad face next to these statements:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say the names of body parts. (My head, my hand)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say the names of clothes. (My hat, my shoes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can explain my matching game to a friend.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite part of making and playing our body and clothes game?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words did you use to describe your body or clothes today?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well during the "Body & Clothes Matching Game" presentations? Were students able to interpret the visual cards and explain the matches clearly using simple English and gestures? Did the mediation activities help them use the vocabulary and grammar accurately? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, simpler vocabulary review) are needed for students who struggled with explaining the game or using gestures?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Head to Toes!" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on the communicative skills developed in this unit.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
    </table>